<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seton Lloyd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Seton Howard Frederick Lloyd</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seton Lloyd was a British archaeologist who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> for much of the 20th century. As adviser to the Iraqi Directorate of Antiquities he excavated Tell Hassuna, Eridu and other sites with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuad-safar">Fuad Safar</a>. He later directed the British Institute of Archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, excavating Beycesultan, and wrote widely read books on the archaeology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Seton Howard Frederick Lloyd was born on 30 May 1902 in Birmingham. He was educated at Uppingham School and studied at the Architectural Association in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, qualifying as an architect in 1926. He gained his first archaeological experience at Tel el <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>, which Henri Frankfort was excavating for the Egypt Exploration Society, and in 1930 Frankfort invited him to join his next excavation, a series of sites in the Diyala valley dug from 1930 to 1937 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Oriental Institute</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1937 to 1939 Lloyd excavated with John Garstang at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mersin">Mersin</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, for the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>. In 1939 he was appointed Archaeological Adviser to the Directorate of Antiquities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where he helped to establish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-iraq">Iraq Museum</a> and to reorganise the Gertrude Bell Museum. He trained Iraqi archaeologists and took part with Iraqi colleagues in several major excavations, notably at '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqair">Uqair</a> and Eridu, at Assyrian Khorsabad and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> at Jerwan. He later succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a> as President of the British School of Archaeology in Iraq.</p><p>In 1948 Lloyd became Director of the British Institute of Archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. With James Mellaart, one of the first scholars at the Ankara school, he excavated the mound at Beycesultan in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and he also conducted excavations at Polatli, Haran, Sultantepe and other Anatolian sites. Michael Gough succeeded him as Director at Ankara. From 1962 to 1969 Lloyd was Professor of Western Asiatic Archaeology at the Institute of Archaeology of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He died at Faringdon on 7 January 1996.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Lloyd published excavation reports with his colleagues, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s Aqueduct at Jerwan with Thorkild Jacobsen (1935), Pre-Sargonid Temples in the Diyala Region with Pinhas Delougaz (1942), the volumes on Beycesultan with Mellaart (1962-1965) and Private Houses and Graves in the Diyala Region (1967).</p><p>He also wrote general accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a> for a wider readership: Ruined Cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> (1942), Twin Rivers: A Brief History of Iraq from the Earliest Times to the Present Day (1943), Foundations in the Dust: A Story of Mesopotamian Exploration (1947, revised in 1980), Early Anatolia (1956), Mounds of the Ancient Near East (1963), Early Highland Peoples of Anatolia (1967) and The Archaeology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from the Old Stone Age to the Persian Conquest (1978). The Interval appeared in 1986 and Ancient Turkey: A Traveller's History of Anatolia in 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seton Lloyd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Excavations by Fuad Safar and <b>Seton Lloyd</b> in 1946 to 1949 uncovered a sequence of some eighteen superimposed temples, growing from a single mud-brick room into a monumental platform shrine.</blockquote><a href="/state/eridu">Eridu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Birmingham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Faringdon, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.48,-1.9&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Birmingham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4507 353 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Seton Lloyd"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4957 623h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4957 623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.48,-1.9&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
